[Chapter "Classical Variation"]
[Line "Main line (6.h4)"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Classical Variation, Lobron System"]

{The most respected main line. Black gives up the centre for quick, free development.} 1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5, so Black can challenge the centre with a pawn while the c8 bishop keeps its diagonal open.} 2. d4 d5 {The point of the Caro-Kann: hit e4 straight away, backed up by the c6 pawn.} 3. Nc3 dxe4 {Take on e4. Black gives up the centre pawn but develops easily.} 4. Nxe4 Bf5 {The Classical Variation: the bishop develops with tempo on the knight.} 5. Ng3 Bg6 {Drop back but stay on the diagonal.} 6. h4 {White threatens h5, which would trap or chase the bishop.} 6... h6 {Gives the bishop a safe square on h7.} 7. Nf3 Nd7 {Controls e5, so White's knight can't jump there and hit the bishop.} 8. h5 Bh7 {The square ...h6 prepared.} 9. Bd3 Bxd3 {Trade White's best attacking piece.} 10. Qxd3 e6 {Complete the pawn chain and open the f8 bishop.} 11. Bd2 Ngf6 {Develop the other knight.} 12. O-O-O Be7 {Black finishes developing. Castling and the ...Qc7 / ...c5 plans come next.} *
